1
00:00:00,310 --> 00:00:05,631
You can be a genuinely winning poker
player — and still go broke.

2
00:00:05,691 --> 00:00:08,605
For one boring, completely avoidable
reason.

3
00:00:08,665 --> 00:00:13,694
In the next few minutes I'll give you the
exact number of buy-ins you need —

4
00:00:13,754 --> 00:00:18,650
cash and tournaments — plus the simple
math the rules of thumb are hiding.

5
00:00:18,710 --> 00:00:23,474
Get it wrong and variance eats your roll
before your edge ever pays off.

6
00:00:23,534 --> 00:00:29,041
Get it right, and a losing month stops
being a crisis and becomes… a non-event.

7
00:00:29,101 --> 00:00:30,957
Let's get your number right.

8
00:00:31,017 --> 00:00:33,187
Everyone's heard the rules.

9
00:00:33,247 --> 00:00:34,244
Twenty buy-ins.

10
00:00:34,304 --> 00:00:35,204
Fifty.

11
00:00:35,224 --> 00:00:37,412
A hundred for tournaments.

12
00:00:37,472 --> 00:00:43,395
Almost nobody can tell you why — or what
actually breaks if you ignore them.

13
00:00:43,455 --> 00:00:46,038
Bankroll management isn't superstition,

14
00:00:46,098 --> 00:00:48,814
and it isn't discipline for its own sake.

15
00:00:48,874 --> 00:00:53,432
It's one specific piece of math with one
specific job:

16
00:00:53,492 --> 00:00:58,226
keeping a real winner alive long enough
for their edge to show up.

17
00:00:58,286 --> 00:01:02,535
By the end you'll know the two anchor
numbers,

18
00:01:02,595 --> 00:01:04,895
the single equation underneath them,

19
00:01:04,955 --> 00:01:08,819
and the three inputs that push your number
up or down.

20
00:01:08,879 --> 00:01:10,867
Start with the headline.

21
00:01:10,927 --> 00:01:14,039
Published guidance clusters around two
numbers.

22
00:01:14,099 --> 00:01:16,219
At least fifty buy-ins for cash.

23
00:01:16,279 --> 00:01:18,598
At least a hundred for tournaments.

24
00:01:18,658 --> 00:01:24,845
Upswing's example is concrete: a
dollar-two game, two-hundred max —

25
00:01:24,905 --> 00:01:29,424
fifty buy-ins is a ten-thousand-dollar
roll dedicated to poker.

26
00:01:29,484 --> 00:01:33,790
For tournaments, PokerNews calls a hundred
buy-ins a minimum —

27
00:01:33,850 --> 00:01:36,689
big-field players hold two to five
hundred.

28
00:01:36,749 --> 00:01:42,673
But here's what nobody says out loud:
those are ceilings of caution, not laws.

29
00:01:42,733 --> 00:01:47,660
Plenty of live regs run leaner — twenty to
thirty buy-ins —

30
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:53,651
because live poker deals so few hands an
hour the swings arrive in slow motion.

31
00:01:53,711 --> 00:01:57,011
That's a personal risk choice,

32
00:01:57,071 --> 00:02:00,980
and it only works if you're a clear winner
who can reload.

33
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:03,764
So the real question isn't the rule.

34
00:02:03,824 --> 00:02:09,495
It's why those numbers… and that starts
with counting the right way.

35
00:02:09,555 --> 00:02:13,887
Quick one: if "measure it" beats "guess
it" to you, subscribe —

36
00:02:13,947 --> 00:02:17,921
I break down the math behind poker like
this every week.

37
00:02:17,981 --> 00:02:21,400
Okay — back to your number.

38
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:24,623
Rule one: stop counting your bankroll in
dollars.

39
00:02:24,683 --> 00:02:29,699
"I have eight grand" tells you nothing
until you divide by the stake.

40
00:02:29,759 --> 00:02:35,298
"I have forty buy-ins at one-two — or
eight at five-ten" — that's a decision.

41
00:02:35,358 --> 00:02:40,115
Counting in buy-ins is what lets you drop
down when you're

42
00:02:40,175 --> 00:02:45,004
running bad and move up when your roll can
take the heat —

43
00:02:45,064 --> 00:02:46,722
without redoing any math.

44
00:02:46,782 --> 00:02:48,308
And it fixes your head.

45
00:02:48,368 --> 00:02:52,338
Losing "two thousand dollars" feels like a
disaster.

46
00:02:52,398 --> 00:02:54,717
Losing "ten buy-ins"… is a Tuesday.

47
00:02:54,777 --> 00:02:57,559
Buy-ins are the natural unit of variance —

48
00:02:57,619 --> 00:03:00,599
which is exactly why every serious
guideline,

49
00:03:00,659 --> 00:03:04,564
and the formula we're about to look at, is
written in them.

50
00:03:04,624 --> 00:03:09,611
And that formula is where the fifty and
the hundred actually come from.

51
00:03:09,671 --> 00:03:12,322
Bankroll rules aren't folklore —

52
00:03:12,382 --> 00:03:16,001
they're a shortcut for one equation: risk
of ruin.

53
00:03:16,061 --> 00:03:20,297
The chance you lose your entire roll
before your edge compounds.

54
00:03:20,357 --> 00:03:22,279
Popularized by Mason Malmuth,

55
00:03:22,339 --> 00:03:26,681
derived properly in Chen and Ankenman's
Mathematics of Poker.

56
00:03:26,741 --> 00:03:31,541
You will never compute this at the table —
and you don't need to.

57
00:03:31,601 --> 00:03:34,857
What matters is what it implies.

58
00:03:34,917 --> 00:03:39,157
Plug in a realistic winner: three big
blinds per hundred hands,

59
00:03:39,217 --> 00:03:44,173
eighty-five standard deviation —
Primedope's own example.

60
00:03:44,233 --> 00:03:49,568
Now watch how fast the required roll drops
as you accept a little more risk.

61
00:03:49,628 --> 00:03:53,660
Thirty-six buy-ins gets you a five-percent
risk of ruin.

62
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:55,907
Forty-eight gets you two percent.

63
00:03:55,967 --> 00:04:02,510
Fifty-six — call it eleven grand at
one-two — gets you to roughly one percent.

64
00:04:02,570 --> 00:04:03,470
That's it.

65
00:04:03,490 --> 00:04:06,491
That's where "fifty buy-ins" comes from —

66
00:04:06,551 --> 00:04:09,711
it's not a tradition, it's a risk setting.

67
00:04:09,771 --> 00:04:15,604
And the equation hands you three levers:
win more per hundred, you need fewer.

68
00:04:15,664 --> 00:04:19,437
Swing harder — higher standard deviation —
you need more.

69
00:04:19,497 --> 00:04:24,462
Demand a lower chance of ever going broke,
you need more again.

70
00:04:24,522 --> 00:04:26,975
Your bankroll isn't one number.

71
00:04:27,035 --> 00:04:30,767
It's the price of the risk tolerance you
pick.

72
00:04:30,827 --> 00:04:35,354
Which finally explains the number that
scares people —

73
00:04:35,414 --> 00:04:38,540
the hundred-plus for tournaments.

74
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:44,462
Now — if you only play cash, you might
think the rest doesn't apply to you.

75
00:04:44,522 --> 00:04:45,453
Stick with me.

76
00:04:45,513 --> 00:04:49,564
The next two parts are the ones that
actually save your bankroll:

77
00:04:49,624 --> 00:04:52,856
why tournaments are a different animal,

78
00:04:52,916 --> 00:04:57,780
and the one assumption every number so far
quietly depends on.

79
00:04:57,840 --> 00:05:00,952
Tournaments need a hundred-plus buy-ins
because

80
00:05:01,012 --> 00:05:03,728
the payout structure is brutal by design.

81
00:05:03,788 --> 00:05:06,900
Only ten to twenty percent of the field
cashes,

82
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:09,543
and the money piles up at the very top.

83
00:05:09,603 --> 00:05:13,769
So you lose small, over and over… and win
big, rarely.

84
00:05:13,829 --> 00:05:17,936
That top-heaviness makes per-tournament
standard deviation

85
00:05:17,996 --> 00:05:21,804
roughly five to ten times a comparable
cash session.

86
00:05:21,864 --> 00:05:24,725
Same skill edge — far wilder ride.

87
00:05:24,785 --> 00:05:27,264
Here's the number that reframes it:

88
00:05:27,324 --> 00:05:31,617
a genuinely winning tournament player —
thirty percent ROI —

89
00:05:31,677 --> 00:05:34,922
can run two hundred or more tournaments
without a

90
00:05:34,982 --> 00:05:38,622
single meaningful cash and still be a
long-term winner.

91
00:05:38,682 --> 00:05:40,869
At a couple of big fields a week,

92
00:05:40,929 --> 00:05:45,032
that's a year-long drought that says
nothing about your skill.

93
00:05:45,092 --> 00:05:47,299
A hundred buy-ins isn't caution.

94
00:05:47,359 --> 00:05:51,505
It's the floor that lets you survive a
normal cold streak.

95
00:05:51,565 --> 00:05:56,858
But every number in this video — cash and
tournaments — leans on one assumption.

96
00:05:56,918 --> 00:05:59,105
And for most players, it's false.

97
00:05:59,165 --> 00:06:01,153
Here's the uncomfortable part.

98
00:06:01,213 --> 00:06:05,521
Every formula we just used takes your win
rate as an input.

99
00:06:05,581 --> 00:06:08,627
And for most players, that number is
negative.

100
00:06:08,687 --> 00:06:12,407
In a University of Hamburg study of online
play,

101
00:06:12,467 --> 00:06:16,471
ninety-one percent of all the rake came
from

102
00:06:16,531 --> 00:06:19,301
the most active ten percent of players.

103
00:06:19,361 --> 00:06:24,150
That only makes sense if the big casual
majority are net losers.

104
00:06:24,210 --> 00:06:27,628
And a peer-reviewed study of over fifty
thousand

105
00:06:27,688 --> 00:06:31,002
players confirmed poker is
skill-predominant —

106
00:06:31,062 --> 00:06:35,879
but results only start reflecting skill
after about a thousand hands.

107
00:06:35,939 --> 00:06:37,862
So the honest sequence flips.

108
00:06:37,922 --> 00:06:41,939
First find out whether you're actually a
winner —

109
00:06:41,999 --> 00:06:46,518
at what rate, with how much variance —
then size the bankroll.

110
00:06:46,578 --> 00:06:51,101
You can't do step two without real data
from step one.

111
00:06:51,161 --> 00:06:53,348
And memory is a terrible logbook.

112
00:06:53,408 --> 00:06:57,907
That means tracking every session —
buy-in, cash-out, hours, stake —

113
00:06:57,967 --> 00:07:02,335
so your win rate and standard deviation
are measured, not guessed.

114
00:07:02,395 --> 00:07:05,980
The math is only ever as good as the
numbers you feed it.

115
00:07:06,040 --> 00:07:11,858
So here's your move: don't pick a bankroll
off a rule of thumb.

116
00:07:11,918 --> 00:07:15,148
Track your next twenty or thirty sessions,

117
00:07:15,208 --> 00:07:18,769
get your real win rate and standard
deviation,

118
00:07:18,829 --> 00:07:22,770
and let the risk-of-ruin math hand you
your number.

119
00:07:22,830 --> 00:07:27,591
Get the buy-in count right, measure your
real numbers,

120
00:07:27,651 --> 00:07:30,480
and let variance do what variance does.

121
00:07:30,540 --> 00:07:38,152
Now — if a tool can compute your win rate,
it can also help you study.

122
00:07:38,212 --> 00:07:41,992
And that's exactly where the AI hype gets
loudest.

123
00:07:42,052 --> 00:07:44,775
So can AI actually solve poker?

124
00:07:44,835 --> 00:07:50,666
I answer that one right here — and it's
not what the ads are selling.

125
00:07:50,726 --> 00:07:57,519
Short version: AI already beat the world's
best players — and it wasn't ChatGPT.

126
00:07:57,579 --> 00:08:00,911
The full story's in that video.

127
00:08:00,971 --> 00:08:03,695
Measure your numbers first.

128
00:08:03,755 --> 00:08:04,744
Then go watch it.

